.TH TIMBER\-OTLP 1 "2026-09-02" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timber\-otlp \- ship a timberfs entry stream to an OTLP/HTTP receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timberfs follower create
.I NAME
.B \-\-select
.I EXPR
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\- timber\-otlp \-\-endpoint
.I URL
.br
.B timberfs feed \-\-select
.I EXPR
.B \-\- timber\-otlp \-\-endpoint
.I URL
.br
.B timberfs query \-\-records
.I STORE
.B | timber\-otlp \-\-dry\-run
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timber\-otlp
posts log ENTRIES \(em a stamped line plus its continuation lines, so a
stack trace stays one record \(em to an OTLP/HTTP receiver: an
OpenTelemetry Collector, or any backend that speaks OTLP. One
.B LogRecord
per entry.
.PP
It is a
.BR CONSUMER .
It reads a
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
stream on
.B standard input
and writes the consumer protocol back on
.BR "standard output" ,
which means it is
.I fed:
by a registered follower
.RB ( "timberfs follower run" ),
by
.B timberfs feed
directly, or by a pipe for a one-off. It holds no position of its own,
opens no store, and needs no access to one \(em so the same binary ships
a store on this host and, run under
.BR ssh (1),
one on another.
.PP
The
.B store is the send buffer:
where a collector's queue is sized by guessing, retention is the
disconnection budget \(em
.B retain 30d
means the receiver can be gone for thirty days \(em and any window can be
re-shipped afterwards, which a collector cannot do because it retains
nothing.
.SH WHAT IT REPORTS
timberfs owns the positions;
.B timber\-otlp
says how far each may move. Its reports are WATERMARKS, meaning \(lqdo
not send me these again\(rq \(em never \(lqthese were delivered\(rq, and
that distinction is what makes the two failures behave differently:
.TP
.B The receiver is down
Nothing is reported. The position stays, the entries come again on the
next attempt, and the backlog accumulates in the store where retention
already has a budget for it. Retried forever, so a receiver that comes
back loses nothing.
.TP
.B The receiver refuses them permanently
The position is reported PAST them, and a
.B note
says so. A 4xx that is not retryable will be a 4xx again, so holding the
position would stop this follower for good and lose everything after it
too \(em a worse outcome than losing the batch, said loudly rather than
silently.
.PP
A note reaches
.B timberfs follower status
and is kept with the position, so \(lqthe collector refused 12 records at
14:02\(rq survives a restart of both ends and is answerable without the
journal. No error taxonomy crosses the boundary: what a receiver's answer
MEANT is knowable here and nowhere else, which is why nothing but a
position and a sentence goes back.
.PP
Delivery is
.B at-least-once
\(em all OTLP offers anyway, having no deduplication key. A batch is
reported only after the receiver accepts it, so a crash re-delivers
rather than skips.
.SH WHERE A RESOURCE COMES FROM
Each store's entries carry its identity, and its labels arrive with them:
the feeder emits a
.B source
record whenever the stream joins a new store or a store's labels change,
so the resource attributes are handed over rather than looked up. One
request carries a
.B ResourceLogs
group PER STORE, so a selection of four hundred stores is never flattened
into whichever one the stream opened with.
.PP
A
.B source
is also a FLUSH boundary: entries before it belong to the previous store
and are sent under the previous resource.
.SH MAPPING
.TP
.B time
.B timeUnixNano
is the entry's own logline stamp, falling back to arrival when the line
carries none;
.B observedTimeUnixNano
is always the write time of the chunk it arrived in. OTLP's two time
fields are timberfs's two axes.
.TP
.B body
The entry's bytes verbatim, minus the trailing newline. Multiline entries
stay one body.
.TP
.B severity
The first UPPERCASE level word in the entry's FIRST line
(TRACE/DEBUG/INFO/NOTICE/WARN/ERROR/FATAL/CRITICAL and friends), mapped
to the OTLP severity numbers. Case-sensitive on purpose: \(lqconnection
error\(rq is not an ERROR. Other conventions need
.BR \-\-severity\-regex .
.TP
.B resource
What the STORE is:
.B service.name
from
.BR \-\-service ,
else the store's
.B service
label, else its name; plus
.BR host.name ,
.B timberfs.store.id
and
.BR timberfs.store.path .
.B \-\-resource
overrides any of them.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Destination
.TP
.BI \-\-endpoint " URL"
A base URL (\fB/v1/logs\fR is appended) or the signal URL itself.
Default:
.BR $OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT ,
.BR $OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT ,
else
.BR http://localhost:4318 .
Plaintext
.B http
only: terminate TLS in a collector or proxy beside it.
.TP
.BI \-\-header " K=V"
Extra request header (repeatable), e.g.
.BR "\-\-header 'Authorization=Bearer x'" .
.B $OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS
is also read, which is where a secret belongs \(em a follower's
declaration is world-readable on purpose.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " DUR"
Connect/read/write timeout per request (default 10s).
.SS What is sent
.TP
.BI \-\-service " NAME"
Resource
.BR service.name .
Given here it overrides EVERY store's own, which is rarely what a
selection wants: left out, each store answers for itself.
.TP
.BI \-\-resource " K=V"
Extra resource attribute (repeatable); overrides derived ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-severity\-regex " PATTERN"
Where the level is, when it is not an uppercase level word. The first
capture group (else the whole match) becomes
.BR severityText ;
a name that maps to no OTLP level keeps the text and claims no number.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-size " N"
Maximum LogRecords per export request (default 512).
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-timeout " DUR"
Send a partial batch after this long with nothing new (default 1s).
.TP
.BI \-\-encoding " proto|json"
Wire encoding (default
.BR proto ,
what every OTLP sender defaults to and what a receiver is likeliest to
accept).
.B json
is readable on the wire; both carry the same request.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " none|gzip"
Compress request bodies (default
.BR none ).
Worth it over a network, noise over loopback.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the export requests instead of sending them, always in the JSON
spelling \(em the two encodings carry the same request, and one of them is
readable.
.IP
⚠ On
.BR STDERR .
Stdout is the report channel and a flag must not change what a file
descriptor means: printed there, the dump interleaves with the reports and
the feeder rejects the stream on the first unmarked byte. So the way to
read it is
.B \-\-dry\-run 2>&1 >/dev/null | jq .
\(em and it is printed even under
.BR \-\-quiet ,
being what was asked for rather than a progress note.
.IP
The entries are still reported TAKEN, which is honest: a watermark says
\(lqdo not send me these again\(rq, and printing them twice is what it
prevents. To preview without remembering anything, run the feeder with no
positions file.
.SS Other
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress progress notes on standard error; errors still print.
.SH DELIVERY
A retryable answer (429, 502, 503, 504, or a transport failure) is retried
with capped exponential backoff, honouring
.BR Retry\-After ,
forever: the store holds the backlog, and giving up would turn a
reachable-again receiver into lost data. A streak is reported once rather
than once per attempt. Any other 4xx/5xx is permanent per the OTLP
specification \(em the batch is dropped, loudly, and reported past (see
WHAT IT REPORTS).
.B partialSuccess
inside a 200 is counted and noted; it carries a count and no identities,
so there is no subset to retry.
.SH FILTERING
Downstream of the reporter, never upstream. What reaches
.B timber\-otlp
is what the follower's position will move past, so a filter placed BEFORE
it would hold the position at every dropped record and stall the store;
placed after it \(em in the collector, or in a program of your own reading
this one's output \(em it drops what it likes with the position already
honestly reported. A consumer that discards a record and reports it taken
has told timberfs the truth, which is the only thing timberfs asks of it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the stream ended cleanly \(em including a FOLLOWED stream whose
feeder stopped, which is an ordinary shutdown and not a truncation.
Non-zero on a configuration error, a truncated record stream, or a
standard output that cannot be written (nobody is listening for the
reports).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ship every apache store on the host to one collector \(em one process,
whatever the store count, and a store created tomorrow is picked up
without touching this:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timberfs list \-\-select '[service=~apache\-.*]'   # what it will ship
timberfs follower create collector \\
    \-\-select '[service=~apache\-.*]' \-\-enable \-\-start \\
    \-\- timber\-otlp \-\-endpoint http://collector:4318
timberfs follower status collector
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same thing in the foreground, to try it out (no positions file, so it
remembers nothing):
.PP
.RS
.EX
timberfs feed \-\-follow \-\-select '[service=~apache\-.*]' \\
    \-\- timber\-otlp \-\-endpoint http://collector:4318
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ship to a collector on another machine, with no transport to configure:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timberfs follower create remote \-\-select '[]' \-\-enable \-\-start \\
    \-\- ssh relay01 timber\-otlp \-\-endpoint http://localhost:4318
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replay an incident window \(em a deliberate act, so it is a bounded query
piped in rather than a mode of the shipper:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timberfs query backing/app.log \-\-records \\
    \-\-from '2026\-08\-11 14:00' \-\-to '2026\-08\-11 15:00' \\
  | timber\-otlp \-\-endpoint http://new\-backend:4318
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what would be sent, without a receiver:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timberfs query backing/app.log \-\-records \-\-tail 20 \\
  | timber\-otlp \-\-dry\-run 2>&1 >/dev/null | jq .
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timber\-filter (1),
.BR timberfs\-records (5),
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5)
